      text: `Long before piped water reached homes, the dry regions of western India depended on stepwells to survive the months without rain. A stepwell is a deep trench lined with stone stairs that lead down to the water table. As the water level fell during summer, people simply walked further down the steps to collect it. Many stepwells were far more than practical structures. Wealthy patrons, often women, paid for them as acts of charity, and builders decorated the walls with carved pillars, balconies and figures. Because the lower levels stayed several degrees cooler than the surface, travellers rested there during the hottest part of the day, and villagers gathered to talk and celebrate festivals. During the colonial period, however, many stepwells were abandoned. Officials considered them unhygienic, and new pumps and pipes made them seem unnecessary. Rubbish filled some, while others collapsed from neglect. In recent years, local groups have begun cleaning and restoring them, not only as tourist attractions but as a way of recharging groundwater. Their revival reminds us that older solutions to scarcity may still hold lessons for the present.`,
