      text: `Many students believe that staying up late to study is a sign of hard work. Research on sleep suggests the opposite. During sleep, the brain does not simply switch off; it actively sorts and stores the information gathered during the day. Experiments have shown that people who sleep well after learning a new skill perform it better the next morning than those who stay awake. Sleep also affects the body. A person who regularly sleeps fewer than six hours a night is more likely to catch infections, gain weight and suffer from high blood pressure. Lack of sleep slows reaction time as much as alcohol does, which is why tired drivers are a serious danger on the roads. Teenagers face a particular difficulty. Their natural body clock shifts during adolescence, making them feel sleepy later at night, yet school often begins early in the morning. Experts recommend simple habits to improve sleep: going to bed at the same time each night, avoiding mobile screens for an hour before bed and keeping the bedroom dark and cool. A rested mind, they argue, is a student's most valuable tool.`,
