      text: `Jagadish Chandra Bose, born in 1858 in what is now Bangladesh, was one of the first Indian scientists to win recognition abroad. After studying in Kolkata and later at Cambridge, he returned to teach physics at Presidency College. At first, he was paid only a fraction of the salary given to his European colleagues. In protest, he refused to accept any salary for three years, until the college finally agreed to pay him equally. In the 1890s, Bose carried out pioneering experiments with very short radio waves, building his own equipment in a small room with the help of a local tinsmith. He showed that such waves could be sent through walls and could trigger a bell at a distance. Remarkably, he chose not to patent most of his inventions, believing that knowledge should be shared freely. Later in life, Bose turned his attention to plants. Using a sensitive instrument he designed, the crescograph, he measured tiny movements in plants and argued that they respond to light, heat and injury in ways that resemble the nervous responses of animals. In 1917 he founded the Bose Institute in Kolkata, which continues his work to this day.`,
