      text: `In many Indian cities, people face a strange paradox. During the monsoon, streets flood and rainwater rushes into drains and out to the sea. A few months later, the same neighbourhoods queue for water from tankers, and wells run dry. Rainwater harvesting offers a way to break this cycle. The idea is simple: instead of letting rain run off, people collect it from rooftops and either store it in tanks or direct it into the ground through recharge pits. A house with a roof of one hundred square metres can capture about eighty thousand litres in a season of average rainfall, enough to meet a large share of a family's needs. The practice is not new. Villages in Rajasthan have long used covered underground tanks, while ancient towns built reservoirs to catch every drop. What is new is the urgency. As cities grow and concrete covers open land, less water soaks into the soil, and groundwater levels keep falling. Several cities have made rainwater harvesting compulsory for new buildings. However, rules alone are not enough. Systems must be cleaned before each monsoon, or the pits become clogged with silt and stop working.`,
