      text: `Every day, thousands of people arrive in India's large cities with a bag of clothes and the hope of a better life. Most of them come from villages where farming no longer provides enough work for growing families. In the city, they find jobs on construction sites, in factories, in markets and in households. Their labour builds the flyovers and apartment towers that change the skyline, and the money they send home pays for their parents' medicines and their siblings' schooling. Yet migrants often live on the edge of the city in more than one sense. Many cannot afford rent in established neighbourhoods and settle in crowded informal colonies with poor sanitation. Because they frequently move between jobs, they may struggle to obtain ration cards or enrol their children in school. When work disappears, as it does during sudden economic shocks, they have little to fall back on. Planners increasingly recognise that cities cannot function without migrant workers and that ignoring their needs is short-sighted. Affordable rental housing near workplaces, documents that can be used anywhere in the country and better public transport are among the measures now being discussed.`,
